{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>Behold, I send an angel before you.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat), the subject of this passage is that the Holy One, blessed be He, informed Moses that Israel would sin with the calf, and therefore He said 'for he will not bear your transgression' (לפשעכם) in the singular, and did not say 'for your sins' (לחטאתיכם). And therefore He said to them that He would send an angel with them, since they were not worthy that the Shekhinah go with them." "<b>And behold,</b> Moses our master, peace be upon him, through the power of his prayer annulled this, so that it was not fulfilled in his days, but it was fulfilled after him, in the days of Joshua, when this angel returned to his place. The sense of לשמרך בדרך, 'to guard you on the way,' is that he would guard them on the way through the desert. And the sense of אשר הכינותי, 'which I have prepared,' is, for My dwelling, in the manner of (Exodus 15:17) מכון לשבתך פעלת — 'the foundation of Your dwelling You have made,' which is the Holy Temple; and it is the same expression that Solomon used (I Chronicles 29:19) ולבנות הבירה אשר הכינותי — 'and to build the citadel which I have prepared.' And this angel, according to the opinion of our master Hananel, of blessed memory, was Michael, the great prince, in the manner that Gabriel said to Daniel (Daniel 12:1): ובעת ההיא יעמוד מיכאל השר הגדול העומד על בני עמך, and it is written (ibid. 10): ואין אחד מתחזק עמי על אלה כי אם מיכאל שרכם. And he it was who appeared to Joshua, and he it was who said to him (Joshua 5:14): אני שר צבא ה' עתה באתי — 'I am the prince of the host of YHWH; now I have come.' And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, explained that the meaning of 'for My name is within him' (כי שמי בקרבו) is that he is called 'the prince of the host of YHWH,' in the manner of what is written (I Kings 8:43): כי שמך נקרא על הבית הזה — 'for Your name is called upon this house,' which is called the house of YHWH. And the meaning of 'for he will not bear your transgression' (כי לא ישא לפשעכם) is: you must beware of him, lest you sin, for even though I am sending him, I am not sending him to bear iniquity, for I am the One who bears and pardons, in the manner of what is written (Isaiah 43:25): אנכי אנכי הוא מוחה פשעיך — end of quote. This too is the opinion of R. Abraham, of blessed memory, that this angel is Michael." "<b>And I saw</b> one of the masters of the Kabbalah who does not reject this view, and says that it is close to reason that this angel should be Michael, for he is appointed over forgiveness, for he is the prince of ḥesed; and from this comes 'for he will not bear your transgression,' that is, even though he is appointed over this, all the more so [he will not] do what he is not appointed over. And he is the seraph that appeared to Isaiah, of whom Scripture mentions (Isaiah 6): ויעף אלי אחד מן השרפים ובידו רצפה, and it is clear that it was Michael, for our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded in the first chapter of Berakhot that what is said of Michael is greater than what is said of Gabriel, for of Michael it is written ויעף אלי אחד מן השרפים, and of Gabriel it is written (Daniel 9) והאיש גבריאל אשר ראיתי בחזון בתחלה מועף ביעף. And they say there in the Gemara: what proves that this verse speaks of Michael? For it is written (Daniel 10) והנה מיכאל אחד השרים הראשונים וגו׳, and Rabbi Yohanan said: the word 'one' (אחד) is derived from 'one' — here it is written ויעף אלי אחד מן השרפים, and it is written והנה מיכאל אחד מן השרים. And the reason Scripture called Michael a seraph, when he is of water, is according to the matter: because there was in his hand a coal (רצפה) of fire, and furthermore, because the attribute of mercy was transformed into the attribute of judgment, which is what is written (Isaiah 6): לך ואמרת לעם הזה שמעו שמוע וגו׳ השמן לב העם הזה וגו׳, and this is what was said to him (ibid.): וסר עונך וחטאתך תכופר — and this is proof that he is appointed over forgiveness. And because of this, the angel in this passage is the seraph in Isaiah, and therefore it is mentioned here: כי לא ישא לפשעכם, and the intention is to say that even regarding that over which he is appointed, he has no authority to act without My permission. And it is possible to say that this view is hinted at here in His saying כי ילך מלאכי לפניך." "<b>And behold,</b> the decree of this angel was annulled all the days of Moses, for Moses annulled it through the power of his prayer, that Israel not be delivered into the hand of an angel all the days of Moses' life, for he filled the place of the angel. But after Moses' death, the angel returned to his place, which is what is written (Joshua 5): אני שר צבא ה' עתה באתי, and this is Michael, for he is one of the four camps of the Shekhinah, and he is the prince of the host of a camp. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: עתה באתי — 'now I have come' — 'I am the one who came in the days of Moses your master, and he pushed me away and did not want me to go,' and so forth." "<b>And by</b> way of the Midrash: הנה אנכי שולח מלאך לפניך — if you had merited, I Myself would have become your emissary, in the manner that I became for you in the wilderness, as it is said (Exodus 13): וה' הולך לפניהם יומם. But now that you have not merited, I deliver you to an emissary — hence הנה אנכי שולח מלאך לפניך. You find that at the hour Israel said (Exodus 24) כל אשר דבר ה' נעשה ונשמע, the Holy One, blessed be He, said: I commanded the first man one commandment, that he might fulfill it, and I equated him to the ministering angels, as it is said (Genesis 3): הן האדם היה כאחד ממנו. These, who accepted 613 commandments — is it not logical that they should live and endure forever? (Exodus 32) חרות על הלחות — Rabbi Yehudah and Rabbi Nehemiah disagree: Rabbi Yehudah says: free (חרות) from servitude; Rabbi Nehemiah says: free from the angel of death. And this is what is written (Numbers 21:18–19): וממדבר מתנה וממתנה נחליאל ומנחליאל במות — from the wilderness, the Torah was given as a gift (מתנה) to Israel, and through the Torah that was given as a gift, they inherited El (נחלו אל) forever, ומנחליאל במות — and since through it they were to live forever, death (מות) came. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: in the stubbornness of the first man you went — he did not withstand his trial for three hours — (Psalms 82:7) אני אמרתי אלהים אתם וגו׳ אכן כאדם תמותון — formerly you were sustained by means of the Holy Spirit, and now you are not sustained except by means of an angel — hence הנה אנכי שולח מלאך לפניך. This is what Scripture says (Jeremiah 3): ואנכי אמרתי איך אשיתך בבנים ואתן לך ארץ חמדה נחלת צבי צבאות גוים — the Holy One, blessed be He, said: I and you thought that you would be children and I the father — how could you do this, that I should place the nations of the world between Me and you? For it says איך אשיתך, a term of separation, like (Genesis 30) וישת לו עדרים לבדו. ואתן לך ארץ חמדה — a land that the great ones of the world coveted: Abraham said to the children of Heth (Genesis 23): תנו לי אחזת קבר עמכם; the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Isaac (Genesis 26): גור בארץ הזאת; Jacob said (Genesis 50): בקברי אשר כריתי לי. נחלת צבי — just as this gazelle, its skin cannot hold its flesh, so too the land of Israel cannot hold its fruits. צבאות גוים — a land that the kings of the world desired; how so? It is written (Joshua 12): מלך יריחו אחד מלך העי אשר מצד בית אל אחד — and between Jericho and Ai there is no more than three milim, yet it says this! Rather, every king who reigned outside the land and had no place in the land of Israel was not called a king — why? Because they coveted the land and its fruits. R. Parnakh said in the name of R. Yohanan: see what is written (Joshua 7): וארא בשלל אדרת שנער פורפירא בבלאה — which the king of Babylon wore when he was in Jericho. And He says (Jeremiah 3): אבי תקראי לי — for I brought you out of Egypt and split the sea for you, and you made a calf; I lacked nothing for you, and you betrayed Me. The gentiles betrayed Me, and I gave them princes to guard them; so too you — I am giving you an angel to guard you. Hence: הנה אנכי שולח מלאך." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: 'Behold, I send an angel before you, to guard you.' This angel is not of the separate beings who sin, of whom it is written (Job 4:18) 'and in His angels He places שׂהלה' — and the שׂהלה was because they attributed power to themselves, in the matter of Sodom, when they said (Genesis 13) 'for we are destroyers'; rather, he is of the נטיעות. And this is what they expounded in Midrash Tanchuma: 'כי לא ישא לפשעכם' — because he is of the class that do not sin; and he is Metatron, the Prince of the Countenance. And therefore it says 'to guard you (לשמרך) in the way,' for the Aramaic translation of משמרת is מטרת, and this is the attribute of judgment; and therefore it says 'לפניך' — in the language of (Habakkuk 3:5) 'לפניו ילך דבר ויצא רשף לרגליו'; and it says (Isaiah 63:9) 'כי ילך מלאכי לפניך,' and this is the language of 'מלאכי' — that is, My angel, the beloved one who is with Me, through whom I am known in the world; and of him it is said (Exodus 33:13) 'פני ילכו,' when Moses asked and said 'הודיעני נא את דרכך' — he asked Him for the attribute by which He is known, and He answered him 'פני ילכו,' and this is (Isaiah 63:9) 'ומלאך פניו הושיעם' — an angel who is His countenance. And therefore it says 'וצרתי את צורריך' — through him; and it further says 'והכחדתיו,' for in the attribute of judgment the enemies are blotted out. And the reason he is called an 'angel' though he is not of the separate beings is that the governance of the world is through him, for he is the attribute of judgment; and the reason it says 'השמר מפניו' is that from the attribute of judgment a person must guard himself lest he be punished for his sin, and therefore it used of him the language of guarding and the language of countenance. And it says 'ושמע בקולו' — a warning not to cut down the נטיעות, and therefore it says immediately 'מיד אל תמר בו'; and the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: 'אל תמר בו' — do not exchange Me for him; rather, one should contemplate that all is one and all is unified without division. 'כי לא ישא לפשעכם' — that is, this angel has power to bear sins, and forgiveness is entrusted to his hand, and nevertheless he will not bear your transgression if you rebel against him, for whoever rebels against him rebels against My unified Name that is within him. And what David, of blessed memory, mentioned (Psalm 130:4) 'כי עמך הסליחה,' and the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded 'You did not entrust it to an angel' — this refers to an angel of the separate beings, for 'כי שמי בקרבו' — My unified Name is within him, for ביה ה', and this is what Onkelos translated: 'ארי בשמי מימריה.' And the intent of this is: all that he says, he says only in My Name; and it explains by this that his voice is the voice of El Elyon, and this is what it says: 'כי אם שמוע תשמע בקולו ועשית כל אשר אדבר' — it did not say 'אדבר,' to explain that his voice is the word of HaShem and His will, for through him He speaks — this one influencing and this one influenced; the Sender is the influencer and the angel is the recipient, emanated from Him, just as in the lower creation the woman was emanated from the man, as it says (Genesis 2:23–24) 'לזאת יקרא אשה כי מאיש לוקחה זאת.' And so we find in every place where a מנחה is written in the Torah — it is written 'הוא' and the woman is intended, yet it is written 'הוא' and we read 'היא,' to explain that she is included in 'he.' And so you will find in the first verse: at its beginning ה' and at its end ה', with יו״ד and א״ו between them, and after it ה'. And this matter the Rav, of blessed memory, hinted at in the verse (Leviticus 2) 'כל המנחה.' And when Israel sinned with the calf, this angel withdrew from among them, and this is what the Holy One, blessed be He, said (Exodus 33:3) 'כי לא אעלה בקרבך' — for as long as this angel, in whom the unified Name dwells, walks among Israel, the Holy One, blessed be He, would not say 'כי לא אעלה בקרבך,' for since His Name is within him, He is among Israel. But once he withdrew from them through their sin, the Holy One, blessed be He, desired that one of the separate beings be sent before them — as it says (ibid.) 'ושלחתי לפניך מלאך וגרשתי את הכנעני' וגו' 'כי לא אעלה בקרבך' — and this angel of the separate beings, through whom He promises to drive them out, is the angel Gabriel. And immediately afterward it says (ibid.) 'וישמע העם את הדבר הרע ההזה ויתאבלו' — for they were mourning the withdrawal of this angel, the Prince of the Countenance, in whom the unified Name dwells, and they feared for their lives lest from now on they fall into the hand of enemies with only one of the separate beings." "<b>And with</b> this knowledge of Kabbalah, the words of the Rabbis, of blessed memory, are to be believed, who expounded that all the days of Moses no angel was with them — that is, no commanding-officer angel — for this angel, who is of the נטיעות, walked with them; he is not a commanding-officer angel, for he is not of the separate beings. And what is written 'ושלחתי לפניך מלאך' — and he is of the separate beings — this was not fulfilled, for Moses our master nullified the decree through the power of his prayer, and he himself filled the place of the angel of the separate beings. But after Moses died, the commanding-officer angel returned to his place." "<b>And the opinion</b> of the Ramban, of blessed memory, regarding the commanding-officer angel who returned in the days of Joshua — that he was Gabriel — and Moses all the days of his life filled the place of Gabriel the prince, as hinted in the verse (Exodus 17): 'כאשר ירים משה ידו וגבר ישראל'; and in the days of Joshua he returned to his place to wage the battle of HaShem on behalf of Israel — and he is Gabriel, who fights for them. And therefore he appeared to Joshua with his sword drawn in his hand, as it says (Joshua 5): 'וחרבו שלופה בידו.' And therefore you find there great acts of vengeance in the destruction of the enemies, by means of very novel modes of vengeance, in the manner of what is written (Joshua 10): 'וה' השליך עליהם אבנים גדולות מן השמים עד עזקה וימותו,' as well as in the matter of the destruction of those kings. And the attribute of judgment was stretched out over them to destroy them in body and in soul — something you will not find in the war that Moses waged, where Scripture mentions nothing but the destruction of their bodies and their banishment, in the manner of what is written concerning the war against Sihon and Og (Deuteronomy 3): 'החרם כל עיר מתים הנשים והטף' — and there it does not mention 'soul' at all. But in the wars of Joshua it mentions 'soul' in all his wars and in all the cities he captured — in each and every one it mentions 'soul,' and at the end it says (Joshua 10): 'ואת כל הנשמה החרים,' to explain that not only did he destroy the bodies and consume them, but he also banned the souls, that they should be cut off from the World to Come. And therefore this angel, who is the Prince of Might, needed to go with him, to be over the cities of the enemies in place of the Elohim of the hosts, and they fell before Israel by thousands and by tens of thousands. And may El merit us to see wonders from His Torah."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {5 "Printed source cites Genesis 13 for the Sodom verse 'כי משחיתים אנחנו'; the verse is Genesis 19:13. Also, the word שׂהלה (Job 4:18) appears as תהלה in standard texts; rendered here as printed in the source.", 6 "No note needed."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:04:21.441555079Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}